00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.920
What if a 3D printed part looks

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.240
completely normal, but is actually

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.320
dangerously weak?

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.320
In modern manufacturing, that's not

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.280
science fiction. It's a real cyber

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.160
security risk. Today's industrial 3D

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.320
printers are connected systems. They can

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.600
be monitored remotely, controlled

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.080
remotely, and unfortunately attacked

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.240
remotely, and an attacker doesn't need

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.400
to break the machine. An attacker can

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.600
simply tweak process parameters like

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.559
temperature or speed to quietly weaken a

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.640
part from the inside. The problem is

00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:41.680
traditional inspection methods won't

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.640
always catch this. A part may look fine,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.520
but structurally it could fail. In this

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.440
work, we ask a simple question. Can we

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.440
detect these attacks while a part is

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.600
being printed? To do this, we used

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.680
external sensors measuring temperature,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.840
vibration, and air quality to monitor

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.840
the printer in real time. Then, we

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.239
trained a machine learning model called

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.640
an autoenccoder to learn what normal

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:07.119
printing behavior looks like. When

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:10.080
something unusual happens, the model

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:12.240
flags it as an anomaly. We tested this

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.799
by intentionally modifying the printing

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.040
parameters of a laserbased 3D printer

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:19.680
and producing tensile test specimens

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.759
along with an artifact measurement part.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.080
The tensile specimens were evaluated

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:26.159
using a tensile testing machine, while

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.640
the artifact parts were scanned with a

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:31.759
highresolution 3D scanner to detect

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.360
dimensional deviations. The results were

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:37.360
striking. Some parts lost over 50% of

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.759
their strength, yet still appeared

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.000
nearly identical under high resolution

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:44.799
scanning. Visually, they passed

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.960
inspection. However, the sensor data

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:48.880
told a different story. Our model

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.360
successfully detected many of these

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.600
hidden deviations, identifying when

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.240
process behavior no longer aligned with

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.240
normal operating conditions. This

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.320
demonstrates that cyber security in

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.640
manufacturing is not only about

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.719
protecting data. It is about protecting

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.360
the physical integrity of what we

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:09.520
produce. By combining sensor data with

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.920
machine learning, we can move towards

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.800
smarter systems that monitor themselves

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.959
in real time and detect issues before

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.680
failures propagate across entire

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:22.080
production batches. As manufacturing

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.959
becomes more connected, solutions like

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.360
this can play a key role in ensuring not

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:29.520
just surface level accuracy, but

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.920
consistent internal quality throughout

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.920
what we print.


